<Layout
  title="Projects | Joe Fazio"
  description="Work Joe Fazio has built: a time-banking system, hands-on security labs, a risk assessment, and this site."
>
  <h1>Projects</h1>

  <p>
    Five projects. Four came out of my final year of coursework at James Madison
    University, and the fifth is this site. The employers, dates and courses
    behind them are on my <a href="/resume.pdf">resume</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="acc-timebank">ACC Timebank</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · lead developer · Google Apps Script, Google Sheets, Python</p>

  <p>
    A time-banking system for the Alachua Community Collective, a mutual-aid
    group in Florida whose members trade hours of work instead of money. I was
    lead developer on the fast, low-cost track and did most of the requirements
    gathering across the whole engagement. The build covered transaction logic
    for crediting and debiting hours, admin reporting, a burnout-risk flag, and a
    Python script to migrate existing member records into the new system. It
    appears on my resume as the Quick Hit Timebank System.
  </p>

  <p class="more">
    <a href="/projects/acc-timebank"
      >Read the case study, with code and a security review</a
    >
  </p>

  <h2 id="labs">Ethical Hacking Lab Course</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · JMU · VirtualBox, Kali Linux, Metasploit, Nessus</p>

  <p>
    A semester of hands-on security labs run inside an isolated virtual network I
    built for the purpose. It pairs a Kali Linux machine with a Windows victim on
    a private, host-only adapter with no route to the outside, so every
    experiment stays contained on my own computer and cannot touch anything real.
    Setting that environment up and keeping the two machines talking to each
    other, and only each other, was the first lab and the foundation for the
    rest.
  </p>

  <p>
    From there the labs worked through security principles like defense in depth,
    a keylogger study focused on detection and remedies, social engineering and
    how to counter it, PGP for encrypting communications, hash functions and the
    integrity checks they support, and a Nessus vulnerability scan whose
    deliverable was a triaged, prioritized set of fixes rather than the raw
    findings. That last habit, deciding what to fix first, is the one that
    carries straight into the BRHS assessment below.
  </p>

  <h2>BRHS Cybersecurity Risk Assessment</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · co-author · NIST CSF 2.0, CIS Controls v8</p>

  <p>
    A consulting report for Blue Ridge Health Services, a fictional healthcare
    provider written for the assignment: identify the weaknesses, rank them, and
    recommend hardening a real budget could absorb. We assessed the organization
    against NIST's Cybersecurity Framework rather than inventing our own
    categories, so a reader can check every finding against a published
    standard. This was coursework. The method is the part that carries over.
  </p>

  <h2>Capsim Business Simulation</h2>
  <p class="meta">Spring 2026 · strategic management</p>

  <p>
    A multi-round simulation in which a team runs a company against competitors,
    making pricing, R&amp;D, marketing and production decisions each round and
    carrying the results into the next. I ran the R&amp;D side, revising our
    product designs each round to keep them inside the shifting buying criteria
    for our segments. It was the closest thing in my coursework to deciding with
    incomplete information and a real cost attached to being wrong, and the
    clearest lesson was that a strategy is only worth as much as the
    follow-through behind it each round.
  </p>

  <h2 id="this-site">This site</h2>
  <p class="meta">2026 · ongoing · Astro, Cloudflare Pages, GitHub Actions</p>

  <p>
    A static site built with Astro and hosted on Cloudflare Pages, with DNS and
    TLS I manage myself. Every push runs a build in GitHub Actions, and a branch
    rule blocks anything from reaching the main branch unless that build passes.
    The repository is public and includes a decision log with a written reason
    for every choice in it, including the ones I reversed. Terraform for the
    Cloudflare configuration and a small Cloudflare Worker are the next pieces.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/joefazio1/joefazio-portfolio">Source on GitHub</a>
  </p>
